Page 1 of 4

Shelly-Pop stretches at sunrise before carrying berry cakes to the hilltop picnic. Grey
clouds tell her she cannot waste the morning.
Page 2 of 4

The other walkers hurry down the wide path, but Shelly stays calm enough to notice ants
marching through a crack in the old wall.
Page 3 of 4

Beside the ants, a line of white pebbles marks a safe shortcut through the clover. Shelly
follows it without tipping one cake.
Page 4 of 4

Shelly arrives first and covers the picnic just as rain begins. Slow did not mean late;
careful helped her see what rushing feet had missed.
Questions for Shelly-Pop’s Slow and Steady

Read the story together first. Use one question at each page, then the question at the end. Answers are below
the dotted line: fold them under or cover them while reading.
Page 1

Talk together: Why does Shelly-Pop begin early?
Page 2
Talk together: What does Shelly notice because she stays calm?
Page 3
Talk together: How do the pebbles help Shelly?
Page 4
Talk together: Why does Shelly reach the picnic first?
After reading
What helped Shelly-Pop find the shortcut?
Answers (fold under before reading)

After reading: A line of white pebbles.

